FRONTEND-ONLY MASTER IMPLEMENTATION PROMPT
API Behavior Miner & Business-Logic Vulnerability Tester
Prompt type: Frontend design and implementation only
Project: Final Year Project — Semester 7 (40%) + Semester 8 (60%)
Authoritative requirement source: API_Behavior_Miner_Complete_Frontend_Specification.md and the SRS Version 4.0
Frontend stack: React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS
Delivery boundary: Browser-side application only. Do not implement or modify the backend.
1. ROLE, OBJECTIVE AND FRONTEND-ONLY BOUNDARY
Act as a Principal Frontend Architect, Senior React/TypeScript Engineer, Product UI/UX Designer and Security UX Specialist.

Your task is to design and implement the complete frontend application for API Behavior Miner & Business-Logic Vulnerability Tester. The result must be a polished, professional, responsive and fully navigable security-assessment workspace that follows the provided SRS and Frontend Specification.

This task is strictly limited to the frontend. Your deliverable is the React application and its client-side code, styling, visual states, frontend tests and frontend documentation.
1.1 What you are allowed to do
- Inspect the existing repository to identify the frontend application, package manager, current dependencies, available API client and existing backend API documentation.
- Create and modify files belonging to the frontend application.
- Build routes, pages, layouts, navigation, forms, tables, charts, graph visualizations, drawers, dialogs, menus and reusable components.
- Implement client-side validation, UI state management, loading states, error handling, filtering, sorting, pagination and responsive behavior.
- Integrate with already-existing backend endpoints through a typed frontend API client, when their actual contracts are available.
- Build a clearly isolated mock/demo data adapter only when backend endpoints or data are not yet available.
- Write frontend unit, component and end-to-end tests.
- Document frontend assumptions and missing API contracts without inventing backend behavior.
1.2 What you must NOT do
Do not implement, create or modify:
- FastAPI/Python backend services or backend routes.
- Pydantic request/response models on the server.
- PostgreSQL schemas, SQLAlchemy models or Alembic migrations.
- Redis, Python RQ queues or background worker processes.
- Docker runner, Dockerfiles, Docker Compose services or network isolation.
- OWASP ZAP, Schemathesis, HTTPX or crawler execution logic.
- Authentication server logic, password hashing, session issuance or secret storage.
- Scope enforcement, DNS/IP validation or target-side security testing.
- Backend business rules, finding verification engines or benchmark calculation services.
Do not turn this into a full-stack implementation task. The frontend may consume existing backend APIs, but it must not build those APIs. Do not change backend code simply to make a frontend screen appear functional.
1.3 Product boundary
The frontend is a presentation and user-control interface. It must never send assessment/crawling/security-test traffic directly from browser JavaScript to a target website or API. It sends user actions to the platform's existing control API. The backend and restricted runner remain authoritative for permissions, target authorization, scope validation, budgets, execution, cancellation and evidence verification.

Postman may be used as inspiration for workspace organization, navigation, compact information layout and resource inspection. Do not clone Postman, its branding or its unrelated features. This is an academic FYP platform, not a commercial enterprise API product.
