10. FRONTEND API INTEGRATION — CONSUME, DO NOT BUILD
This is a frontend-only task. Use existing API definitions if present. Do not create new backend endpoints or edit server-side code.
10.1 Centralized API layer
Create or reuse one frontend API client. It should centralize:
- Base URL/configuration.
- Existing authentication/session behavior.
- Typed request/response interfaces.
- Standard error normalization.
- Request cancellation where useful.
- Correlation/request IDs when required by the existing contract.
- Query invalidation/refetch behavior.
Generate or verify TypeScript types from existing OpenAPI contracts when possible. Preserve actual backend field names and enum values. Do not guess endpoint names, request bodies or status enums when a contract is available.
10.2 When backend contracts are missing
Create frontend-only interfaces/adapters and document the unknown fields/endpoints in docs/API_CONTRACT_GAPS.md. Use a clearly isolated mock adapter for UI development. Do not claim that an assumed endpoint exists.
10.3 Mock/demo mode
- Keep mock data isolated from production API logic.
- Show a persistent but subtle DEMO DATA or MOCK MODE label wherever mock records are presented.
- Never mix mock records with live backend data.
- Use deterministic fixtures suitable for screenshots and tests.
- Provide loading, empty, success, error, blocked, forbidden, partial and unknown states.
- Never present a mock scan as an actual security assessment.
10.4 State management
Separate server state, local UI state and authenticated session context. Use query caching for server state when appropriate. Do not put passwords, access tokens, refresh tokens, cookies or target credentials into browser storage.

Map canonical status enums centrally. Preserve the original backend value and provide a safe Unknown fallback for unrecognized values. Never map an unknown state to Success.
11. SAFETY AND SECURITY UX REQUIREMENTS
These rules are mandatory for the frontend:
1. Never send assessment/crawling/security-test traffic directly from the browser to the target.
2. Backend authorization is authoritative.
3. Target registration and target authorization are different states.
4. Display target, environment and scope version before launch.
5. A discovered URL does not automatically become eligible for active testing.
6. Passive/read-only behavior is the default presentation.
7. State-changing operations need explicit approval and disposable/resettable test state where applicable.
8. Do not expose denial-of-service, brute-force, destructive or persistence-oriented options.
9. Cancellation requested is not the same as cancellation confirmed.
10. Do not invent progress percentages, run completion or successful outcomes.
11. Do not store secrets/tokens in local/session storage.
12. Treat API responses, crawler output, filenames and target-controlled strings as untrusted.
13. Render untrusted content as text; sanitize any allowed formatted preview.
14. Bound evidence/log previews and do not load huge response bodies into list pages.
15. Respect project/workspace permissions in every UI query and detail view.
16. Zero findings is not proof of target security.
17. AI suggestions cannot authorize execution or override deterministic results.
18. Never expose privileged runner or Docker control details in the user-facing UI.
The frontend communicates status and collects user intent. It does not replace backend policy enforcement.
12. ASYNC STATES, ACCESSIBILITY AND RESPONSIVENESS
12.1 Async UI states
Every asynchronous page/form/query must handle the states relevant to that interaction:
- Initial loading.
- Refresh/background fetching.
- Empty result.
- Validation error.
- API/network error.
- Access denied.
- Not found.
- Pending mutation.
- Success after confirmed backend response.
- Partial result.
- Offline/reconnecting/stale data where relevant.
- Unknown status.
Do not display success before the server confirms the operation. Preserve user-entered form state on recoverable errors where appropriate.
12.2 Accessibility
Aim for WCAG 2.2 AA quality:
- Semantic landmarks and heading hierarchy.
- Real labels for inputs.
- Keyboard-accessible navigation, dropdowns, tabs and dialogs.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Correct focus handling for dialogs/drawers.
- Error messages associated with the relevant fields.
- Async status announced accessibly where appropriate.
- Adequate text/control contrast.
- Reduced-motion support.
- Do not rely on color alone.
12.3 Responsive behavior
The SRS's initial target is modern desktop browsers. Prioritize desktop because data tables and dependency graphs need space, but implement usable tablet/mobile layouts:
- Wide desktop: full sidebar, header, tables, graph canvas and side panel.
- Laptop: collapsible sidebar and compact tables.
- Tablet: drawer navigation and stacked forms.
- Mobile: readable monitoring, finding and dashboard summaries; complex scope/graph editing can display an honest desktop-recommended notice rather than broken controls.
13. FRONTEND PERFORMANCE EXPECTATIONS
Use the SRS's provisional targets as measured engineering goals, not guaranteed claims:
- Dashboard initial view: provisional target around 2 seconds in the defined local demo profile.
- Dashboard reads: provisional P95 target of 3 seconds under the SRS reference workload.
- Client-side workspace navigation: provisional target around 300 ms.
- Inventory and findings lists: pagination/bounded result windows.
- Charts: use backend aggregates for large data sets and display filter/time range.
- Behavior graph: assess usability around 150 operation nodes and 300 edges.
Use route-level code splitting where helpful, memoize expensive graph elements, avoid unnecessary re-renders and lazy-load large details. Do not load full request/response bodies into tables. Measure on the declared demo hardware before reporting performance.
